#: ../../source/docs/selecting-a-frame.rst:5
#: cf04413801554cfab334660858a11da2
msgid "Selecting a Rover Frame"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/selecting-a-frame.rst:7
#: 8d1735f7fcc0441fa6c31097b10f5352
msgid "Building a Rover-based vehicle project from the ground up is entirely feasible, but it's recommended to start with an existing RC vehicle. Utilizing this approach provides an immediate head start, furnishing you with a pre-assembled frame, complete with wired motor and ESC, thereby streamlining your construction process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/selecting-a-frame.rst:10
#: e6d1a757aa074f79a049a623493decc7
msgid "If you choose to begin with a pre-assembled RC vehicle, the main areas of focus should be the successful integration of key components. These components typically include the autopilot system, GPS module, telemetry radio, and an RC Transmitter. The integration process involves carefully mounting these components on the RC vehicle, ensuring reliable connections, and configuring settings as required."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/selecting-a-frame.rst:14
#: 6f8b2493d0284f60815581a041d49eb1
msgid "Keep in mind that the list of required hardware can vary depending on the complexity and specific requirements of your project. For instance, you might need to incorporate additional sensors for obstacle avoidance, environmental monitoring, or a companion computer for advanced processing tasks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/selecting-a-frame.rst:17
#: 30376c39f8de41bebe4d54ad6fb84ab3
msgid "Moreover, it's important to consider the spatial constraints of your RC vehicle while deciding on these components. Smaller RC vehicles may require more compact hardware or innovative mounting solutions. On the other hand, larger vehicles might offer more freedom in terms of component selection and placement, but could present their own unique challenges."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/selecting-a-frame.rst:26
#: ea3cfc5734eb41e09eb7c550157fa2f3
msgid "Choosing the Right Scale"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/selecting-a-frame.rst:28
#: 04da66e0efac433fb3121c78b9eb7098
msgid "For those leaning towards a classic car design, a 1/10 scale RC car is a superb choice. 1/10 scale vehicles are often recommended due to their sizable dimensions that facilitate easier maintenance and hassle-free installation of supplementary equipment, such as companion computers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/selecting-a-frame.rst:31
#: a2950575fcf64f558793c7e310022fc4
msgid "Although the compactness of smaller-scale vehicles (1/16 or `1/18 scale <https://hobbyking.com/en_us/1-18-brushless-4wd-stadium-truck-w-18amp-system.html>`__) might impose a more challenging setup process, the image gallery on this wiki reassures you that overcoming this challenge is wholly attainable with some effort."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/selecting-a-frame.rst:33
#: 3caf0bf4d25c4cb8969bfe055f111e04
msgid "For those who favor slow, skid-steering rovers, the `Wild Thumper <https://www.robotgear.com.au/Product.aspx/Discontinued/1978-Wild-Thumper-6WD-Chassis-Black-34-1-gear-ratio>`__ models come highly recommended by the community."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/selecting-a-frame.rst:36
#: 793d835370484671a1012c8eef9ead50
msgid "Comparing 2WD and 4WD"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/selecting-a-frame.rst:38
#: 81e737e823f645628cb761ae58e6df24
msgid "Performance-wise, `2WD <https://hobbyking.com/en_us/basher-bsr-bz-222-1-10-2wd-racing-buggy-arr.html>`__ vehicles tend to outperform their `4WD <https://hobbyking.com/en_us/turnigy-trooper-sct-4x4-1-10-brushless-short-course-truck-arr.html>`__ counterparts. This is because their simpler mechanical design results in prolonged battery life compared to an equivalent 2WD model. However, for high-speed cornering or navigation on uneven terrains, 4WD reigns supreme."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/selecting-a-frame.rst:42
#: 85a59b6f44de4f778f1506fd248ce446
msgid "Additional Recommendations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/selecting-a-frame.rst:44
#: 8675bc277ca640ffbf948b2852704f73
msgid "Opting for vehicles that come without RC units is suggested. This recommendation stems from the fact that the RC units bundled with most ready-to-run RC cars often lack the extra channel essential for autopilot mode control and tuning."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/selecting-a-frame.rst:45
#: e136204e5bcc4806a725042daf130cb0
msgid "For increased efficiency, durability, and performance, consider a brushless motor."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/selecting-a-frame.rst:46
#: 156a2e8a0cc5419dad6ee91a6ca5dfbf
msgid "Consider the placement of the battery and motor on the frame. These will most likely be the heaviest components on your rover and having them as low as possible decreases the tendency to roll over."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/selecting-a-frame.rst:47
#: b4d9f03554374cee894c627d4ffad5c6
msgid "Look for a vehicle that offers ease of maintenance and component replacement. You might need to replace or upgrade parts over time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/selecting-a-frame.rst:48
#: 5a2d8ae2d45d41009623bbc7f81122a0
msgid "Think about the power requirements of all components, including the autopilot, to ensure your power supply can handle the load."
msgstr ""
